== De Jonquières subgroup

Any algebraic unipotent subgroup of $"GL"_n (KK)$ is conjugated to a subgroup of the group $U_n (KK)$ of unipotent upper-triangular matrices. What for $Aut(X)$?


  Let $X=AA^n$ and consider a _de Jonquières_ subgroup of unipotent triangular automorphisms:
  $ "Jonq"_u (n, KK)  = { (x_1,...,x_n)|->(x_1+P_1,...,x_n+P_n) | \ P_i in KK[x_1,..,x_(i-1)] , i<=n }. $

It is _nested_, i.e., it is a union of an increasing sequence of algebraic subgroups. It is an exponent of
$ jonq_u (n, KK) := xor.big_i KK[x_1,...,x_(i-1)] partial/(partial x_i) subset "Der" cal(O)(AA^n). $

We define $"Jonq"_u (n, R)$ and $"jonq"_u (n, R)$ by substituting an affine domain $R$ for $KK$.


== Nested unipotent subgroups

#theorem("Zaidenberg-Kraft'24")[
If a nested unipotent subgroup $U subset Aut(AA^n)$ acts transitively on $AA^n$, then it is conjugated to a subgroup of $"Jonq"_u (n, KK)$.
]

Not true without transitivity condition. What for other varieties?


Let $X$ be an irreducible affine algebraic variety. 



#definition[
  A subset $S subset Der(X)$ is called _locally nilpotent_ if for any \ $f in cal(O)(X)$ and $s_1,s_2,... in S$ we have $s_n dot dots.c s_1 f=0$ for some $n in NN$.
]

Results from arXiv:2312.08359 below.

#pagebreak()

#lemma[
  Let $L subset Der(cal(O)(X))$ be a Lie subalgebra contained in $LND(cal(O)(X))$, and $A$ its kernel.
  Then there exist a non-zero $h in A$ and algebraically independent elements $x_1,...,x_k in cal(O)(X)$ over $A$ such that:
  + $A_h$ is finitely generated;
  + $cal(O)(X)_h = A_h [x_1,...,x_k]$;
  //+ $A_h [x_1,...,x_j]$ is stable under $L$ for any $j$.
  + $L$ is a Lie subalgebra of $jonq_u (k, A_h)=plus.o.big_i A_h [x_1, ..., x_(i-1)] partial / (partial x_i).$
  In particular, $L$ is a locally nilpotent subset.
] <lemLstructure>

#proof[
  Let $FF := "Frac"(A)$ be the field of fractions of $A$, and set $B := FF times.o_A cal(O)(X)$.
  Then $L_FF := FF times.o_A L subset "Der"_FF (B)$ is a Lie subalgebra contained in $LND_FF (B)$, and its kernel in $B$ is $FF$.
  By Skutin'21, there exist elements $x_1, dots, x_k$ of $B$, algebraically independent over $FF$, such that $B = FF[x_1, dots, x_k]$ and $L_FF subset.eq xor.big_(i=1)^k FF[x_1, dots, x_(i-1)] partial/(partial x_i)$.

  Let $g in A$ be a common denominator of a finite system of generators of $cal(O)(X)$ expressed as polynomials in $x_1, dots, x_k$ over $FF$.
  There exists a multiple $h in (g) subset A$ such that $A_h$ is finitely generated.
  Then $cal(O)(X)_h = A_h [x_1, dots, x_k]$. So, we have the first and second statements.
  
  Every derivation $partial in L$ equals $sum_j f_j partial / (partial x_j)$ for some $f_j in FF[x_1, dots, x_(j-1)]$.
  Since \ $partial in "Der"(cal(O)(X))$, we have $ f_j = partial(x_j) in cal(O)(X) inter FF[x_1, dots, x_(j-1)] subset A_h [x_1, dots, x_(j-1)]. $ 
  This establishes the last statement.
]

#pagebreak()

#lemma[
  Let $S subset LND(cal(O)(X))$ be a finite locally nilpotent subset.
  Then the Lie subalgebra $W subset Der(cal(O)(X))$ generated by $S$ is finite-dimensional.
]

#proof[
  Let $S = {d_1, ..., d_s}$, and let $f in cal(O)(X)$ be arbitrary.
  Let us call $f in cal(O)(X)$ _bounded_ if there exists $N>0$ such that any composition of $N$ basis elements applied to $f$ yields zero.
  Assume that there exists an unbounded $f in cal(O)(X)$.
  Then  $d_(i_1)(f)$ is unbounded for some $i_1 <= s$.
  Repeating this argument indefinitely produces an infinite sequence $d_(i_1), d_(i_2), ...$ such that $d_(i_N) dots.c d_(i_1) dot f$ is unbounded (and non-zero) for any $N in NN$.
  This contradicts the local nilpotency of $S$.

  Therefore, $W dot f$ is finite-dimensional for any $f in cal(O)(X)$.
  Applying this observation to a finite system of generators of the algebra $cal(O)(X)$, we conclude that $W$ is finite-dimensional itself.
]


#lemma[
  If $U subset Aut(X)$ is a nested unipotent subgroup, then $U= exp L$ for a Lie subalgebra $L$ of $Der(cal(O)(X))$ contained in $LND(cal(O)(X))$.
  The converse also holds.
]

#proof[
  Let us choose any ascending chain of finite-dimensional subspaces\ $V_1 subset V_2 subset dots.c subset L$ such that $L = union.big_i V_i$.
  By local nilpotency, for each $i$ the minimal Lie subalgebra $W_i$ of $L$ containing $V_i$ is finite-dimensional.
  Then $exp(W_i)$ is an algebraic unipotent subgroup and $W_i$ is contained in $W_(i+1)$ for each $i$.
  Thus,\ $U = union.big_i exp(W_i)$ is a nested unipotent subgroup.

  The converse statement is straightforward: if we have $U = union.big_i U_i$, where\ $U_1 subset U_2 subset dots.c$ is an ascending sequence of unipotent algebraic subgroups, then $log(U)$ is a union of Lie subalgebras $log(U_i)$ in $LND(cal(O)(X))$.
]

